     8	A session is delegated when its prompt contains `SESSION_MODE: delegated` or a
     9	`WRITE_SCOPE:` field.
    10	
    11	For every delegated session, `WRITE_SCOPE` is the exhaustive repository write
    12	allowlist. It takes precedence over all repository-local workflow instructions,
    13	including this file's end-of-work checklist and instructions to update
    14	`RUN_STATE.md`, `TASK_QUEUE.md`, plans, reports, generated documentation, or tests.
    15	Those files are lead-owned unless their exact paths are explicitly included in
    16	`WRITE_SCOPE`.
    17	
    18	Repository instructions may require a delegated worker to read or inspect a file,
    19	but they never implicitly authorize writing it. Test failures, consistency repairs,
    20	generated artifacts, tidy-tree work, and work believed necessary for completion do
    21	not expand the allowlist.
    22	
    23	If an unlisted write appears necessary, the worker must preserve that path, complete
    24	independent authorized work, and stop with a blocking scope-expansion request.
    25	
    26	The same early-return rule applies to any blocking decision that is not the
    27	worker's to make — naming, contract semantics, spec conflicts, authority gaps.
    28	Do not guess and do not invent: finish independent authorized work, then stop
    29	with a blocking question (the NEEDS_RULING shape: question, options considered,
    30	recommendation, blocked work). The lead always resumes compliant early-returns
    31	with an answer; a short round-trip is always cheaper than a wrong guess. Only
    32	the lead can approve an expansion through a new or resumed prompt. Approval is
    33	prospective; an already-made out-of-scope edit remains a scope violation.
    34	
    35	For lead sessions without a `WRITE_SCOPE` field, the normal end-of-work
    36	expectations below continue to apply.
    37	
    38	## Intake And Authority
    39	
    40	For any substantial task:
    41	
    42	1. Read the targeted sections of `RUN_STATE.md`: `ACTIVE_STOP_CARD`, Current
    43	   Project Status, Known Workspace State, and What Is Next. An active stop card
    44	   overrides every other restart or queue pointer.
    45	2. Read the Current Queue and Do-Not-Do-Yet sections of `TASK_QUEUE.md`. Triage
    46	   a new user request there before treating it as ordinary next work.
    47	3. Run Mission M0 in `docs/agent_playbook.md`. For delegation, review, or
    48	   multi-stream work, also read `docs/orchestration.md` before landing changes.
    49	4. Consult the owning plan, contract, exit checklist, and targeted decision-log
    50	   entries instead of re-deciding settled policy. The source-of-truth map is in
    51	   `AGENT_PLAN.md`.
    52	5. Inspect `git status --short --branch` and preserve unrelated or untracked
    53	   user work.
    54	
    55	Direct, bounded questions may use a smaller read set, but stop cards, workspace
    56	safety, and machine-state lanes always apply.
    57	
    58	## Machine-State Safety
    59	
    60	A Codex or Claude session is agent load. Never start or continue a
    61	`[QUIET-MAC]` measurement task from this bridge: no production campaigns,
    62	detection-floor captures, powermetrics sessions, or other quiet-window data
    63	collection. Report the gate and hand it back for a clean, lead-controlled
    64	session. `[AGENT]` work is compatible with this bridge; `[ED-EXTERNAL]` work
    65	requires the user.
    66	
    67	Never present fixture-first or mock evidence as live hardware validation.
    68	Preserve PROVISIONAL labels until the owning live gate is actually satisfied.
    69	
    70	## Bidirectional Agent Bridge
    71	
    72	This repository supports one bounded cross-model hop in either direction:
    73	
    74	- Claude Code may start or continue Sol through the project Codex MCP server.
    75	  Such prompts must carry `BRIDGE_ORIGIN: claude` and
    76	  `BRIDGE_HOPS_REMAINING: 0`; the Claude-side MCP configuration also disables
    77	  the reverse Claude server inside that Sol session.
    78	- A top-level Codex session may request a read-only Fable judgment consult via
    79	  the project Claude MCP `consult_fable` tool and
    80	  `.agents/skills/claude-consult`. Contract §8 owns the reverse-consult wire
    81	  rules. The project allowlists and preapproves only this consult tool.
    82	
    83	Never delegate final verification or authority decisions. The top-level caller
    84	remains lead and adjudicates the peer's advice.
    85	
    86	The ONE home for bridge wire policy is `docs/contracts/bridge_protocol.md`
    87	(`bridge-protocol/v1.1`); Claude-side launch procedure lives in
    88	`.claude/skills/codex/SKILL.md`. Receiver-side enforcement remains explicit:
    89	
    90	- `WRITE_SCOPE` is exhaustive; never infer additional scope from tests, generated files, repository instructions, or work believed necessary for completion.
    91	- Never start or continue a `[QUIET-MAC]` measurement while an agent session is active.
    92	- Never use `danger-full-access` or sandbox/approval bypass flags.
    93	- Bridge depth is one hop: a Claude-originated Sol session must not call Claude by MCP, `claude -p`, or any other launcher.
    94	- A missing, duplicated, malformed, or non-final required envelope is protocol failure, never success.
    95	
    96	When a ruling or scope expansion is required, stop and use the contract's
    97	early-return shape. The lead applies and verifies peer advice and all final
    98	changes.
    99	
   100	## Work And Verification
   101	
   102	- Follow the repository contracts and keep raw evidence and run bundles
   103	  immutable.
   104	- Prefer bounded, reviewable changes. Do not commit, push, merge, deploy, or
   105	  perform destructive Git operations unless the user explicitly requests it or
   106	  the current lead instructions clearly grant that authority.
   107	- Use the narrowest safe sandbox and request approval for actions that need
   108	  more access.
