docs/paper/round7/structural-edits.md:198:> | **Phase accounting:** do the two phase energies close to request energy, stay invariant to later work, preserve timing-term provenance, meet the accepted calibration band and sampling rules, and show phase-edge dominance? | Per bundle, \(D_i=E_{\mathrm{prefill}}+E_{\mathrm{decode}}-E_{\mathrm{request}}\). Fit prefill energy against later output tokens and compose its slope interval. Separately report the shared `b_fiducial_s` and each member's local/edge term; compare both bracket bounds with the named acceptance edition's band; compute the quarter-window and cadence/sample-flag rates; evaluate the attribution-limited predicate (the artifact's field name is `admissible_set_uncertainty_dominates_point_floor`) for each floor cell. | Overcount: \(\max D_i\le10^{-6}\) J. Undercount: \(\lvert D_i\rvert\le t_{\mathrm{gap},i}P_{\mathrm{gap,max},i}\). Invariance: the entire slope interval lies within both \(L_H=\max_i(H_i)/(T_{\max}-T_{\min})\) and \(L_F=F_{\mathrm{op}}/(T_{\max}-T_{\min})\). Two timing terms must occupy separate fields; both bracket bounds must lie in the acceptance edition's band; both flag rates must equal \(0\); the dominance predicate must be true for every floor cell. | Three prompt/output shapes—\(2048/128\), \(512/512\), \(128/2048\)—with eight admitted bundles each: \(n=24\); two bracket captures; all 24 claim-bearing members for each flag rate; every floor cell (minimum one) for dominance. | Accept iff every listed predicate holds. Missing gap evidence or an earlier \(F_{\mathrm{op}}\) makes its criterion indeterminate. An evaluated closure, invariance, bracket, flag, or dominance failure contradicts the row; a false dominance predicate withholds the attribution-limited label. |
docs/paper/round7/structural-edits.md:202:| **Phase accounting:** do the two phase energies close to request energy, stay invariant to later work, preserve timing-term provenance, and meet the accepted calibration band and sampling rules? | Per bundle, \(D_i=E_{\mathrm{prefill}}+E_{\mathrm{decode}}-E_{\mathrm{request}}\). Fit prefill energy against later output tokens and compose its slope interval. Separately report the shared `b_fiducial_s` and each member's local/edge term; compare both bracket bounds with the named acceptance edition's band; compute the quarter-window and cadence/sample-flag rates; and report the older code-generated diagnostic label for each floor cell. The artifact calls that label *attribution-limited* when its legacy `admissible_set_uncertainty_dominates_point_floor` predicate is true; the label records whether its older corner term exceeded its guarded point term and does not select the paper's result. In this paper, *phase-edge dominance* instead means that every component's complete unguarded corner-re-evaluation ratio \(R\) is at least 2 and no required comparative shared-error ratio \(R_{cm}\) is below 2; §4 builds both ratios. | Overcount: \(\max D_i\le10^{-6}\) J. Undercount: \(\lvert D_i\rvert\le t_{\mathrm{gap},i}P_{\mathrm{gap,max},i}\). Invariance: the entire slope interval lies within both \(L_H=\max_i(H_i)/(T_{\max}-T_{\min})\) and \(L_F=F_{\mathrm{op}}/(T_{\max}-T_{\min})\). Two timing terms must occupy separate fields; both bracket bounds must lie in the acceptance edition's band; and both flag rates must equal \(0\). | Three prompt/output shapes—\(2048/128\), \(512/512\), \(128/2048\)—with eight admitted bundles each: \(n=24\); two bracket captures; and all 24 claim-bearing members for each flag rate. Every floor cell reports its diagnostic label and its separately governed ratios. | Accept iff the closure, invariance, bracket, and flag predicates hold. Missing gap evidence or an earlier \(F_{\mathrm{op}}\) makes its criterion indeterminate. An evaluated closure, invariance, bracket, or flag failure contradicts the row. The diagnostic label neither accepts nor refuses this characterization row; the ratios govern the separate boundary-doubling result and contingent subtitle. |
docs/paper/round7/structural-edits.md:206:## S13 — Reframe the retained ratios and legacy predicate — draft line 103
docs/paper/round7/structural-edits.md:212:> **Diagnostic-era evidence of the phenomenon, not a current instrument property.** In three retained cells, point floors from run-to-run scatter alone were \(0.2888\), \(0.4934\), and \(0.3113\) J; the corresponding corner-widened floors were \(3.153\), \(2.922\), and \(2.184\) J. The paired ratios were \(10.92\), \(5.92\), and \(7.02\). Forcing all timing-envelope widths to zero flipped the registered dominance predicate from true to false, showing that phase-edge placement, rather than the predicate itself, produced the widening.
