    53	## S03 — Add the comparative shared-error replay — before draft line 187
    54	
    55	**Disposition coverage:** R2 and SF-2's mechanism premise.
    56	
    57	**Frozen anchor quote (verbatim; draft line 187):**
    58	
    59	> Passing the identical-condition null block at the corner-widened resolution bound tests the bound itself, and §6 reports that null number first.
    60	
    61	**Ready insertion immediately before the anchor:** The comparative shared-error replay starts from each authenticated four-run A1/B1/B2/A2 block before its timing width is collapsed into one number. First compute the observed block difference \(\delta_j=(B_1+B_2-A_1-A_2)/2\) from the four admitted point energies. Then reintegrate those same four retained power traces while moving all four phase starts together through every registered start-edge candidate and holding the ends fixed; the resulting block differences are the onset sweep \(O_j\). Repeat while moving all four ends together and holding the starts fixed; those block differences are the offset sweep \(P_j\). Both sweeps include the no-shift value \(z_j\), obtained by reintegrating the four traces at their recorded phase edges. Define
    62	
    63	\[
    64	d_j^-=(\min O_j-z_j)+(\min P_j-z_j),\qquad
    65	d_j^+=(\max O_j-z_j)+(\max P_j-z_j),
    66	\]
    67	
    68	and let \(q_j=\max(|d_j^-|,|d_j^+|)+|z_j-\delta_j|\), with only an outward floating-point pad added by the registered implementation. In plain words, \(q_j\) is the farther shared start-plus-end excursion, plus any recorded discrepancy between the reintegrated no-shift value and the authenticated block difference.
    69	
    70	For each of the four block members, also reintegrate that member after moving only its remaining bundle-local clock/edge uncertainty, with the shared fiducial term set to zero; the largest absolute energy change is that member's residual half-width \(r_{jm}\). At this pre-collapse replay stage these four residual half-widths are the four member half-widths \(h_i\) used by §4, so \(\ell_j=(r_{j1}+r_{j2}+r_{j3}+r_{j4})/2=h_{\delta,j}=(h_1+h_2+h_3+h_4)/2\). Thus \(\ell_j\) is a replay name for §4's existing block-difference half-width, not an added uncertainty.
    71	
    72	It then rebuilds the block difference as
    73	
    74	\[
    75	\delta'_j=\delta_j+s q_j+e_j\ell_j,
    76	\]
    77	
    78	where the one shared sign \(s\in\{-1,+1\}\) is the same for every block and each local sign \(e_j\in\{-1,+1\}\) is chosen independently. The replay enumerates both shared signs and every combination of local signs. At every such corner it recalculates the complete comparative unguarded floor—both the largest absolute block difference and the prediction expression built from the new mean and sample standard deviation—and retains the maximum. Dividing that maximum by the comparative point-only unguarded floor gives \(R_{cm}\). A comparative \(R_{cm}<2\) withdraws the boundary-dominance sentence even when independent-edge \(R\ge2\).
    79	
    80	**Worked retained-fixture pass (fixture evidence, not a round-7 campaign result):** In retained block `b01`, \(\delta_1=z_1=0.2146256513\) J. The onset sweep runs from \(0.1098764207\) to \(0.2243993676\) J and the offset sweep from \(0.0576055478\) to \(0.3349382543\) J, so \(d_1^-=-0.2617693342\) J, \(d_1^+=0.1300863192\) J, and \(q_1=0.2617693342\) J after outward rounding. Its four residual half-widths are \(0.0015589205\), \(0.0337198644\), \(0.0491358083\), and \(0.0127439131\) J, giving \(\ell_1=0.0485792531\) J. The retained second block gives \(q_2=0.6153099135\) J and \(\ell_2=0.1356776459\) J. Enumerating the two shared signs and four local-sign combinations produces a point comparative unguarded floor of \(2.4305766103\) J and a maximum shared-error unguarded floor of \(8.8304376431\) J, hence \(R_{cm}=8.8304376431/2.4305766103=3.6330628732\), which passes the threshold of 2. These numbers come from `tests/fixtures/fcm_r4_real_blocks/measured_pair.json` and demonstrate the registered arithmetic only.
    81	
