    99	
   100	The single largest value, 28.932935 ms, is pulse 9's onset. Onsets carry the
   101	larger excursion in 52 of the 59 pulses.
   102	
   103	## How much of the bound is repeatable, and how much is scatter
   104	
   105	The published bound decomposes into four terms that sum to it exactly:
   106	
   107	| Term | Value (ms) | Share | What it is |
   108	|---|---|---|---|
   109	| repeatable onset bias | 13.000000 | 43.2 % | the median onset lag — the part present on every pulse |
   110	| excess of the worst pulse | 14.000000 | 46.6 % | how much further pulse 9's onset lagged than the median pulse (+27.0 − +13.0) |
   111	| reach of the allowed interval | 1.932935 | 6.4 % | how far pulse 9's allowed interval extends beyond its own best fit (28.932935 − 27.0) |
   112	| clock-anchor term | 1.134997 | 3.8 % | the separate bound on placing the whole trace on the wall clock |
   113	| **total** | **30.067932** | 100 % | `B_fiducial`, matching the draft digit for digit |
   114	
   115	**In one sentence: a repeatable bias accounts for 13.0 ms of the 30.07 ms bound
   116	(43 %), and pulse-to-pulse scatter for a slightly larger 14.0 ms (47 %), so the
   117	bias is the largest single term but does not by itself dominate the bound.**
   118	
   119	The distinction that matters is between the typical edge and the bound. For a
   120	typical onset the bias is nearly the whole story: it is 13.0 ms of the 14.924429 ms
   121	median onset excursion, or 87 %. But the bound is a maximum, not a typical value,
   122	and a maximum is set precisely where the scatter is largest. Removing a
   123	well-characterised 13 ms bias would therefore shrink the typical edge error by
   124	roughly seven-eighths while shrinking the bound by well under half.
   125	
   126	The reviewers' stated expectation entering this analysis was a bias of about
   127	+16 ms on onsets and −11 ms on offsets. Those two figures are the values stored
   128	for pulse 0 under the *earlier* estimator, not the distribution medians under the
   129	current one. Re-derived under the current estimator across all 59 pulses, the
   130	medians are **+13.0 ms and −5.5 ms**. The direction of both expectations holds;
   131	the offset magnitude is about half what was expected.
   132	
   133	## Independence: what the pulse-index behaviour shows
   134	
   135	The paper's "95/95" label treats the 59 pulses as independent draws from one
   136	distribution. The numbers below are what the capture shows about that. They are
   137	**descriptive only** — no significance test, model, or confidence statement is
   138	attached to any of them, and none is offered as proof of independence. They are
   139	reported so a reader can see the pattern rather than take the assumption on
   140	trust.
